<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin-bottom: 10px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> International English</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> What Communication Skills Are Important in the Workplace?</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create narratives based on hypothetical scenarios. Interactive: Engage in writing exchanges about future aspirations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create narratives imagining future careers, highlighting the importance of English in global settings (e.g., "As a diplomat, I'll use English to communicate with people worldwide.")<br>Can participate in writing exchanges about future career goals, describing how English will be beneficial and including relevant workplace contexts and detailed examples (e.g., "Learning English will help me communicate in an international business career by allowing me to negotiate contracts, participate in meetings, and collaborate with international partners.")<br>Can write letters to classmates describing aspirations, mentioning English as a key skill (e.g., "I want to become a doctor and use English to read medical research from around the world.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of diverse professionals (e.g., a scientist, a business person, a diplomat) interacting in a global setting. Students are asked to briefly write down two communication skills they think these people *must* possess to succeed in international workplaces. They should also consider how English has become a *lingua franca* in these contexts. (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a concise statement about a communication skill, using target vocabulary. For example, "As an international business person, one *must* have strong *cross-cultural communication* skills. English *has become* the primary language for these *interactions*." The teacher highlights the present perfect and modal usage, and models the pronunciation of words like "relevant" /'rɛl.ə.vənt/ and "necessary" /'nɛs.ə.sɛr.i/, focusing on the long /e/ sound, and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in "communication" /kəˌmjuː.nɪˈkeɪ.ʃən/.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are you writing about one communication skill or two?" (Two)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Should your answer include how English is used in a global workplace, or just general skills?" (How English is used in a global workplace)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International English".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students read a short blog post excerpt titled "English: The Key to Global Career Opportunities" (narrative excerpt, formal register, online platform article format). The text describes how English *has spread* and *become* the *dominant* language in various *global* *workplaces*, from *business* to *diplomacy*, and how *fluency* and *cross-cultural communication skills* are *vital* for *employees* to *connect* with diverse *backgrounds* and *access* *academic resources*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student is able to recognize the importance of English as a global language in professional contexts and identify relevant communication skills for international workplaces.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (written):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the text silently and then write down three reasons why English is considered a *global* or *international* *lingua franca* according to the article (Gist question).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students complete True/False statements based on the text and correct the false ones in writing. E.g., "English has become important only in the business sector." (False: "English has become important in various global workplaces, from business to diplomacy.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Writing.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using the present perfect, modals for necessity ("must," "could," "should," "can"), and indirect questions, integrating vocabulary related to workplace communication skills and English's global role.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze/Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences related to global careers and English, containing blanks to be filled with target vocabulary (e.g., *fluency, accessible, enhance, cross-cultural communication, global interactions*) and correct forms of verbs (present perfect, modals). E.g., "English ____ (become) a _______ language, which ____ (help) us ____ (connect) with *employees* from diverse *backgrounds*." Students individually complete these sentences in writing. (5 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Guided Sentence/Paragraph Writing):</b> Students receive sentence starters and a list of target words (e.g., *diplomacy, business, academic resources, vital, practice, express*) and grammar points. They must write 2-3 sentences about a hypothetical career path, explaining how English *could* or *should* be used. E.g., "If I become a diplomat, I _____ (modal + verb) English to _____ (verb) important ideas and _____ (verb) with international partners. English *has been* key to accessing global *resources*." (7 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check):</b> Students exchange their written sentences/paragraphs from activity 2 with a partner. They read each other's work and provide written feedback focusing on: 1) correct use of present perfect and modals, 2) inclusion of at least three target vocabulary words, and 3) clarity of ideas. Partners also check for spelling, especially words containing the long /e/ sound (e.g., "relevant," "necessary"). (5 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">17 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International English".</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product – A formal letter to a classmate.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students will write a letter to a classmate, describing their future career aspirations. In the letter, they *must* imagine what profession they want to pursue and explain in detail how English *will be a vital skill* for success in that role, using *relevant workplace contexts*. They *should* include examples of how they will use English for *global interactions*, *cross-cultural communication*, and *accessing academic resources*. They *could* also include an indirect question to their classmate. The letter *must* incorporate at least three instances of the present perfect and three different modals of necessity. (e.g., "Dear [Classmate's Name], I hope this letter finds you well. I've often wondered what career path I'll choose, and I've decided I want to become an environmental scientist. English *has become* absolutely *necessary* for this field. I *must* be able to read cutting-edge *academic resources* from around the world. Also, I *could* use English to *communicate* with *global* partners on international research projects. *Can you explain* which *skills* you think are most *important* for your future *workplace*? Sincerely, [Your Name]")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, collaborative interaction (through the letter format), and communicative task completion. Students are encouraged to use formal language in appropriate contexts and adapt their message for a classmate while maintaining a professional aspiration.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment based on a simple rubric.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their completed letters (from Stage 4) with a different classmate. Using a provided checklist, they assess each other's letters.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Included a clear career aspiration.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explained how English is *vital* for the chosen career with specific examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least 3 present perfect sentences correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least 3 different modals of necessity ("must," "could," "should," "can") correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporated at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., *global, workplaces, communication, fluency, business, diplomacy, cross-cultural communication, academic resources*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall clarity and coherence of the letter.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing about future careers and English today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I *can* now describe how English *could* be *beneficial* in a *global* *workplace* by..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing about your future career and the role of English did you find most challenging, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *can* practicing English writing *enhance* your overall *communication skills* for future *workplaces*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The writing activities effectively reinforced the target grammar and vocabulary within the theme of international communication. Students engaged well with the narrative writing and letter format. Some students needed additional scaffolding for structuring their ideas and ensuring consistent use of the present perfect. I *should* consider providing more sentence frames or a more detailed outline for future writing tasks to *enhance* their *fluency* and confidence. The pronunciation focus on the long /e/ sound was noted, and I *could* integrate a quick spelling check activity in the next lesson to reinforce this connection in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will summarize their written aspirations from this lesson and verbally present them to a new audience, integrating their writing into a comprehensive project that includes speaking and listening skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>